    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">Plot a hundred thousand stars by temperature and brightness and they do not
    scatter: ninety per cent of them fall on one diagonal band. That band is not a kind of star, it is a
    <em>stage</em> &mdash; every star fusing hydrogen in its core sits on it, and leaves it when the
    hydrogen runs out. Hertzsprung and Russell found this independently, 1911 and 1913, and it remains the
    single most useful diagram in astronomy. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> established science.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Spectral Classes <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>O, B, A, F, G, K, M</h3>
      <p>The sequence is by surface temperature, from roughly 40,000 K down to 2,400 K, and it is an accident
      of history that the letters are not alphabetical: Annie Jump Cannon reordered Pickering's hydrogen-line
      classes at Harvard around 1901 when it became clear that temperature, not hydrogen strength, was the
      real variable.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>O</strong> &mdash; blue, 30,000 K and up, a hundred thousand times the Sun's output, dead within a few million years. Perhaps one star in three million.</li>
        <li><strong>B</strong> &mdash; blue-white; Rigel, Spica.</li>
        <li><strong>A</strong> &mdash; white; Sirius, Vega, Deneb.</li>
        <li><strong>F</strong> &mdash; yellow-white; Procyon, Polaris.</li>
        <li><strong>G</strong> &mdash; yellow; the Sun, 5,772 K, Alpha Centauri A. About 7% of stars.</li>
        <li><strong>K</strong> &mdash; orange; Arcturus, Epsilon Eridani. Long-lived and increasingly the favourite place to look for life.</li>
        <li><strong>M</strong> &mdash; red dwarfs, under 3,700 K; <em>three quarters of every star in the galaxy</em>, and not one of them visible to the naked eye.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small">Below M run the brown dwarfs &mdash; classes L, T and Y &mdash; which never
      reached the 0.08 solar masses needed to fuse hydrogen. A Y dwarf can be cooler than a cup of tea.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Lives <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>How Long a Star Gets</h3>
      <p>Lifetime goes roughly as mass divided by luminosity, and luminosity goes as something near the
      3.5th power of mass. The arithmetic is merciless: doubling the mass of a star cuts its life by a
      factor of about ten.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li>A 60-solar-mass O star: three million years.</li>
        <li>The Sun, one solar mass: ten billion years, of which 4.6 are gone.</li>
        <li>A 0.1-solar-mass red dwarf: trillions of years &mdash; longer than the present age of the universe by two orders of magnitude, so <em>not one has ever died</em>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last fact has a consequence worth sitting with. The universe is 13.8 billion years old and the
      most common kind of star in it is still in its infancy. If life is a thing that takes time, the
      universe has barely started. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and contested &mdash; a
      reasonable inference, not a result.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Ends <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Three Doors Out</h3>
      <p>What a star leaves behind is set almost entirely by the mass of its core when fusion stops.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>Under ~8 solar masses</strong> &mdash; it swells to a red giant, sheds its outer layers as a planetary nebula (named by Herschel, nothing to do with planets) and leaves a <strong>white dwarf</strong>: a carbon-oxygen cinder the size of the Earth, held up by electron degeneracy pressure, cooling for the rest of time. Limit 1.4 solar masses, Chandrasekhar, 1930 &mdash; worked out at nineteen, on the boat to England.</li>
        <li><strong>8 to ~25 solar masses</strong> &mdash; core collapse, a type II supernova, and a <strong>neutron star</strong>: 1.4 to 2.2 solar masses inside a ball 20 km across, spinning up to 716 times a second. A sugar cube of it weighs as much as a mountain.</li>
        <li><strong>Above that</strong> &mdash; nothing stops the collapse. A <strong>black hole</strong>, treated in its own section below.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small">Every atom in you heavier than helium was made inside one of these objects or
      in the collision of two of them. That is not a metaphor; it is nucleosynthesis.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Variables and Pairs <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Stars That Change, and Stars in Company</h3>
      <p>Most stars are not alone. Something like half of Sun-like stars are in binaries or higher
      multiples, and the figure rises with mass; among O stars, nearly all.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>Cepheid variables</strong> pulse with a period set by their luminosity &mdash; Henrietta Swan Leavitt, 1908, from 1,777 variables in the Magellanic Clouds. This is the rung of the distance ladder that let Hubble measure Andromeda and end the Great Debate.</li>
        <li><strong>RR Lyrae</strong> stars do the same job for older populations.</li>
        <li><strong>Eclipsing binaries</strong> give masses and radii directly, which is where the whole mass scale is anchored.</li>
        <li><strong>Novae</strong> are a white dwarf stealing hydrogen from a companion until the stolen layer detonates. The dwarf survives and does it again.</li>
        <li><strong>Type Ia supernovae</strong> are the same theft taken to the Chandrasekhar limit. They go off at nearly the same brightness every time, which makes them standard candles &mdash; and which is how the acceleration of the expansion was found in 1998.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

