    <p class="plate-note">A cold cloud of gas and dust collapses, spins up because angular momentum is
    conserved, flattens into a disc because that is what spinning collapse does, and lights a star at the
    centre. Dust grains stick, become pebbles, become planetesimals, become worlds; what is left is swept
    up or thrown out. The discs were predicted long before they were photographed &mdash; and then ALMA
    photographed HL Tauri's in 2014, gaps and all. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> established science.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Our Own <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>One Star, Eight Planets, a Great Deal of Rubble</h3>
      <p>Four small rocky worlds inside the frost line; four giants outside it &mdash; two gas (Jupiter,
      Saturn) and two ice (Uranus, Neptune, which are mostly water, ammonia and methane under crushing
      pressure, and should never have been called gas giants). Between and beyond: the asteroid belt, the
      Kuiper belt, the scattered disc, the Oort cloud.</p>
      <p>The Sun is 99.86% of the mass. Jupiter is most of the rest, and its position has shaped
      everything &mdash; probably including the delivery of water to the Earth and the architecture of the
      inner system. Pluto was reclassified in 2006 not out of spite but because Eris was found and the
      choice was to have eight planets or an indefinite number.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Census <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Nearly Six Thousand Others</h3>
      <p>51 Pegasi b, 1995: a Jupiter-mass planet four days from its star. Nobody had a theory that allowed
      it, and the first response of the field was to doubt the measurement. Kepler, launched 2009, then
      found thousands by watching for the 0.01% dip as a planet crossed its star's face; TESS and the radial
      velocity surveys continue, and JWST is now reading the chemistry of their atmospheres.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>Hot Jupiters</strong> &mdash; giants in days-long orbits; rare in truth, over-represented early because they are the easiest to see.</li>
        <li><strong>Super-Earths and sub-Neptunes</strong> &mdash; between Earth and Neptune in size, the <em>most common kind of planet in the galaxy</em>, and we have none.</li>
        <li><strong>Compact multiples</strong> &mdash; TRAPPIST-1: seven Earth-sized worlds round a red dwarf, all of them inside Mercury's orbit.</li>
        <li><strong>Rogue planets</strong> &mdash; thrown out of their systems, drifting unlit between the stars, and possibly outnumbering the bound ones.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small">Best current estimate: most stars have planets, and something like one in five
      Sun-like stars has a roughly Earth-sized planet in the temperate zone. The error bars on that figure
      are large and honest. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and contested.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame caution mt-2">
      <p><strong>On the habitable zone.</strong> It means only that liquid water could persist on a surface
      at that distance, given an atmosphere we have assumed. It is a crude first filter, not a verdict:
      Venus is in the Sun's zone. The order files "a planet in the habitable zone" as
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> interesting, and "a planet where something lives" as
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> unestablished, until a biosignature survives peer review.
      The contact question itself is held at <a href="/extraterrestrials/">Limb VII</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

