          <ellipse cx="380" cy="210" rx="14" ry="5"/>
          <path d="M394 210 C414 206 416 226 396 224"/>
          <path d="M366 210 C346 214 344 194 364 196"/>
          <ellipse cx="450" cy="210" rx="14" ry="5"/>
          <path d="M464 210 C492 202 492 232 458 226"/>
          <path d="M436 210 C408 218 408 188 442 194"/>
          <ellipse cx="516" cy="210" rx="13" ry="5"/>
          <path d="M529 210 C556 196 556 236 512 224"/>
        </g>
        <g font-family="Cinzel, serif" font-size="9" fill="#cbbb93" letter-spacing="2">
          <text x="78" y="168" text-anchor="middle">E0</text>
          <text x="190" y="168" text-anchor="middle">E6</text>
          <text x="250" y="120" text-anchor="middle">S0</text>
          <text x="380" y="40" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7fae7a">Sa</text>
          <text x="450" y="40" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7fae7a">Sb</text>
          <text x="516" y="40" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7fae7a">Sc</text>
          <text x="380" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fb6d8">SBa</text>
          <text x="450" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fb6d8">SBb</text>
          <text x="516" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fb6d8">SBc</text>
          <text x="140" y="110" text-anchor="middle">ELLIPTICALS</text>
          <text x="300" y="268" text-anchor="middle">BARRED SPIRALS &#8212; THE MILKY WAY IS SBbc</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">Hubble's 1926 classification, drawn as the tuning fork of 1936. It is a
    description of shape and nothing more: he thought galaxies evolved along it from left to right, and
    they do not &mdash; if anything the arrow runs the other way, with ellipticals built by mergers of
    spirals. A good taxonomy that outlived its own wrong theory, which is the ordinary fate of good
    taxonomies. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span> scholarship and history.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Kinds <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Four Shapes and Their Habits</h3>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><strong>Spirals</strong> &mdash; a rotating disc of gas, dust and young blue stars with a bulge of old ones; arms are density waves, not solid structures, so stars drift through them like traffic through a jam. Still making stars.</li>
        <li><strong>Barred spirals</strong> &mdash; about two thirds of spirals, ours among them; the bar funnels gas inward and feeds the centre.</li>
        <li><strong>Ellipticals</strong> &mdash; from dwarfs to the monsters at cluster cores; little gas, little dust, almost no new stars, orbits pointing every which way. Built by merger.</li>
        <li><strong>Lenticulars and irregulars</strong> &mdash; the discs that have run out of gas, and the small torn things like the Magellanic Clouds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small">Scale: a dwarf spheroidal may hold a thousand stars; IC 1101 holds perhaps a
      hundred trillion. "Galaxy" covers eleven orders of magnitude of mass.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Active Ones <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Quasars, Seyferts, Blazars, Radio Galaxies</h3>
      <p>In a few per cent of galaxies the central black hole is eating, and the infalling matter outshines
      every star in the host combined. These were catalogued as four different kinds of object before it
      was realised they are mostly <em>the same object seen from different angles</em> &mdash; the
      unification model: looking down the jet gives a blazar, looking across the torus gives a Seyfert 2,
      looking past it gives a quasar.</p>
      <p>3C 273, the first quasar recognised as such (Schmidt, 1963), sits 2.4 billion light years away and
      is still magnitude 12.9. Because they are visible across the universe, quasars are how the early
      universe is surveyed at all.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Missing Mass <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Rotation Curves, and the Argument They Started</h3>
      <p>Vera Rubin and Kent Ford, through the 1970s, measured how fast the outskirts of spiral galaxies
      turn. By Kepler the speed should fall off with distance from the mass. It does not: it flattens.
      Either there is five times more mass than we can see, arranged in a halo, or gravity itself is not
      what we think at low accelerations.</p>
      <p>The evidence has gone heavily one way. Gravitational lensing, the acoustic peaks in the cosmic
      microwave background, and above all the Bullet Cluster &mdash; where the lensing mass walked straight
      through the collision while the visible gas stalled &mdash; are hard to answer with modified gravity.
      But no dark matter particle has been detected in sixty years of looking, and that is not nothing.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> the observations. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and
      contested &mdash; what the missing mass <em>is</em>.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">The Larger Pattern <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Groups, Clusters, Filaments, Voids</h3>
      <p>Galaxies are not scattered evenly. They sit in groups of dozens and clusters of thousands; the
      clusters sit on filaments of dark matter and hot gas; the filaments wall off voids hundreds of
      millions of light years across with almost nothing in them. The whole is called the cosmic web, and
      it grew from density ripples of one part in a hundred thousand, which are visible, directly, in the
      microwave background.</p>
      <p>Our address: the Milky Way, in the Local Group, falling towards the Virgo Cluster, in the
      Laniakea Supercluster &mdash; defined in 2014 by Tully and colleagues not by where things are but by
      which way they are flowing.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================ BLACK HOLES =============================== -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Fourth Subject</p>
    <h2 id="t-the-four-classes-of-black-hole">The Four Classes of Black Hole</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Not a hole and not made of anything. A region where the escape velocity exceeds
    the speed of light, which is to say a region whose future points inward.</p>
  </div>

  <figure id="fig-bh" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate IV</span> The Anatomy of a Black Hole
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="Cross-section of a rotating black hole: singularity, event horizon, ergosphere, photon sphere, innermost stable circular orbit, accretion disc and polar jets">
        <g stroke="#8fb6d8" stroke-width="1" fill="none" opacity="0.7">
          <ellipse cx="280" cy="150" rx="210" ry="26"/>
          <ellipse cx="280" cy="150" rx="150" ry="19"/>
        </g>
        <path d="M274 120 L268 20 L292 20 L286 120 Z" fill="#7fae7a" opacity="0.35"/>
        <path d="M274 180 L268 280 L292 280 L286 180 Z" fill="#7fae7a" opacity="0.35"/>
        <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="96" fill="none" stroke="#9f947a" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="4 5"/>
        <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="68" fill="none" stroke="#c98b6a" stroke-width="0.9" stroke-dasharray="2 4"/>
        <ellipse cx="280" cy="150" rx="56" ry="44" fill="none" stroke="#d7b05a" stroke-width="1"/>
        <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="38" fill="#0e0c0a" stroke="#f3ddaa" stroke-width="1.6"/>
        <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="2.5" fill="#f3ddaa"/>
        <g font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="10" fill="#cbbb93">
          <text x="286" y="146">singularity</text>
          <text x="330" y="196" fill="#f3ddaa">event horizon</text>
          <text x="352" y="214" fill="#d7b05a">ergosphere</text>
          <text x="372" y="96" fill="#c98b6a">photon sphere &#8212; 1.5 r&#8347;</text>
          <text x="392" y="66" fill="#9f947a">innermost stable orbit &#8212; 3 r&#8347;</text>
          <text x="470" y="140" fill="#8fb6d8" text-anchor="middle">accretion disc</text>
          <text x="280" y="16" fill="#7fae7a" text-anchor="middle">RELATIVISTIC JET</text>
        </g>
        <g stroke="#9f947a" stroke-width="0.6" opacity="0.8">
          <path d="M318 190 L298 168"/>
          <path d="M348 208 L306 176"/>
          <path d="M368 92 L322 124"/>
          <path d="M388 62 L330 112"/>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">The horizon is not a surface; nothing is there. It is the last place from which
    light can still get out, at the Schwarzschild radius &mdash; 3 km per solar mass. Inside a rotating
    hole the ergosphere drags spacetime round with it so hard that standing still is impossible, and
    Penrose showed in 1969 that energy can be stolen from that rotation. The photon sphere at 1.5 radii is
    where light orbits; it is the bright ring the Event Horizon Telescope photographed round M87* in 2019
    and Sgr A* in 2022. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> established science.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Class I <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Stellar-Mass &mdash; 3 to 150 Suns</h3>
      <p>The remnant of a massive star whose core could not be held up. Cygnus X-1, identified in 1971, was
      the first that convinced people &mdash; and the subject of a bet Hawking placed against himself and
      lost in 1990. A handful of dozens are known in our galaxy by their X-rays; a hundred million are
      expected to exist here, almost all of them dark and undetectable.</p>
      <p>Since 2015 they are also <em>heard</em>: LIGO's detection of GW150914, two holes of 36 and 29 solar
      masses merging 1.3 billion light years away, released three solar masses as pure spacetime ripple in a
      fifth of a second. Over two hundred mergers are now catalogued.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Class II <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Intermediate &mdash; 100 to 100,000 Suns</h3>
      <p>The awkward middle, long suspected and poorly evidenced. Candidates appear as ultraluminous X-ray
      sources in other galaxies and at the centres of some globular clusters, and GW190521 &mdash; a merger
      producing a 142-solar-mass remnant &mdash; is the cleanest case yet, because that mass falls in the
      range core collapse is not supposed to produce.</p>
      <p>Why it matters: if the supermassive holes grew from stellar ones, this is the rung they climbed.
      If there is no rung, they had to start large. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and
      contested &mdash; real objects, uncertain population.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Class III <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Supermassive &mdash; Millions to Tens of Billions</h3>
      <p>One at the centre of essentially every large galaxy. Sagittarius A* is 4.3 million solar masses,
      a fact established by watching individual stars orbit an invisible point for thirty years &mdash;
      Ghez and Genzel, Nobel 2020. S2 passes within 120 AU at 3% of light speed, and its orbit precesses by
      exactly the amount general relativity demands.</p>
      <p>M87* is 6.5 billion solar masses, larger across than Neptune's orbit, and was the first ever
      photographed. The galaxy-black hole mass relation (M&ndash;&sigma;) is tight enough that the two must
      have grown together &mdash; <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> how, exactly, is unsettled, and JWST
      finding quasars already a billion suns heavy at 700 million years after the Big Bang has made it
      sharply worse.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="frame card reveal">
      <p class="tag">Class IV <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Primordial &mdash; Hypothetical</h3>
      <p>Hawking and Zel'dovich proposed that density fluctuations in the first second could have collapsed
      directly into holes of any mass at all, from a mountain to a star. None has been observed. Most of the
      mass window has been closed by microlensing surveys and by the absence of the gamma-ray burst a small
      one would give as it finished evaporating.</p>
      <p>A narrow window remains open, which is why they are still discussed as a dark matter candidate.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and contested &mdash; a respectable hypothesis with no
      detection behind it.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame mt-2">
      <h3 id="t-evaporation-and-the-information-question">Evaporation, and the Information Question</h3>
      <p>Hawking, 1974: quantum field theory near a horizon makes a black hole glow, faintly, at a
      temperature inversely proportional to its mass. A solar-mass hole radiates at 60 nanokelvin &mdash;
      colder than the microwave background, so in this era it absorbs more than it emits and grows. Left in
      an empty universe it would take 10<sup>67</sup> years to evaporate. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
      established theory; never observed, and not plausibly observable.</p>
      <p>The paradox that follows is the hardest open problem in physics: if the hole evaporates into
      thermal radiation, the information that fell in is destroyed, and quantum mechanics forbids that.
      The current best answer &mdash; that the entropy of the radiation follows a curve that turns over
      (the Page curve), recovered since 2019 from the "island" calculations &mdash; is genuine progress and
      is not a resolution. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> speculative and contested. The order's
      <a href="/decoherence/">Limb XI&middot;i</a> takes up what this means for identity; nothing on this
      page licenses that reading.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================ HOW WE KNOW =============================== -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-how-any-of-this-is-known">How Any of This Is Known</h2>
    <p>Every distance in this page rests on a ladder, and each rung is calibrated by the one below it. Kick
    out a rung and everything above it moves.</p>
    <ol class="tight">
      <li><strong>Radar and spacecraft</strong> fix the astronomical unit to the metre.</li>
      <li><strong>Parallax</strong> &mdash; the shift of a nearby star against the far ones as the Earth crosses its orbit. Bessel got 61 Cygni in 1838; Gaia has now done it for nearly two billion stars.</li>
      <li><strong>Spectroscopic and cluster fitting</strong> &mdash; put a star on the main sequence, compare apparent with absolute brightness.</li>
      <li><strong>Cepheids and RR Lyrae</strong> &mdash; period gives luminosity, out to about 100 million light years.</li>
      <li><strong>Type Ia supernovae</strong> &mdash; standard candles to billions.</li>
      <li><strong>Redshift</strong> &mdash; and Hubble's law, for everything beyond.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-1">There is an open crack in it. The expansion rate measured locally up the ladder
    (~73 km/s/Mpc) disagrees with the rate inferred from the microwave background (~67) by far more than
    either side's error bars allow. This is the <strong>Hubble tension</strong>, it has not gone away with
    better data, and it is the most likely place for the next real surprise.
    <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> the measurements. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> the explanation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== THE LEDGER ============================== -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The Ledger</p>
    <h2 id="t-what-holds-up-and-what-does-not">What Holds Up, and What Does Not</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Survives</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Stellar nucleosynthesis.</strong> The heavy elements in your body were assembled in stars and in neutron-star collisions. Measured in spectra, in meteorites and in the 2017 kilonova GW170817, which made gold in front of us.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>The expansion of space, and its acceleration.</strong> Redshift, supernovae, the microwave background and baryon acoustic oscillations all agree.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Black holes as real objects.</strong> Orbits, X-ray binaries, gravitational waves and two photographs.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Planets are ordinary.</strong> Nearly six thousand confirmed; most stars have them.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">&#9670;</span> <strong>Ancient astronomy was real astronomy.</strong> Babylonian ephemerides, the Antikythera mechanism, Mayan Venus tables, Newgrange and Stonehenge aligned to the solstices. Precise, predictive, and done without a telescope.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Does Not Survive</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>"Astronomers have found a planet exactly like Earth."</strong> They have not. They have found planets of similar radius in similar orbits, with unknown atmospheres.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>Planetary alignments causing anything on Earth.</strong> The tidal pull of Jupiter at closest approach is smaller than that of the building you are in.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">&#9670;</span> <strong>The dark side of the Moon.</strong> There is a far side. Every part of it gets sunlight; it is tidal locking, not shadow.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">&#9670;</span> <strong>Stars that "are already dead".</strong> Almost every naked-eye star is within a few thousand light years and alive. The line is poetry, not astronomy.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>Nibiru, Planet X as catastrophe.</strong> A genuine ninth-planet hypothesis exists for a distant body perturbing Kuiper belt orbits &mdash; and it is an ordinary, cold, far-off world, not an arrival.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== TIMELINE ================================ -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-a-line-through-the-subject">A Line Through the Subject</h2>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li><strong>c. 1600 BCE</strong> &mdash; the Nebra sky disc; Babylonian records begin in earnest, and run unbroken for a millennium. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>c. 240 BCE</strong> &mdash; Eratosthenes measures the Earth with a stick and a well, to within a few per cent. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>c. 150 CE</strong> &mdash; Ptolemy's <em>Almagest</em>: wrong model, superb data, authoritative for 1,400 years. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1543</strong> &mdash; Copernicus moves the centre. <strong>1609</strong> &mdash; Kepler's ellipses; Galileo's telescope finds Jupiter's moons. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1687</strong> &mdash; Newton explains all of it with one law. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1838</strong> &mdash; Bessel measures the first stellar parallax. The stars acquire distances. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1908</strong> &mdash; Leavitt's period&ndash;luminosity law. <strong>1924</strong> &mdash; Hubble resolves Cepheids in Andromeda: other galaxies exist. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1929</strong> &mdash; recession proportional to distance. <strong>1931</strong> &mdash; Lemaître's primeval atom. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1964</strong> &mdash; Penzias and Wilson find the microwave background while cleaning pigeon droppings out of a horn antenna. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1967</strong> &mdash; Jocelyn Bell Burnell finds pulsars. The Nobel went to her supervisor. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>1995</strong> &mdash; 51 Pegasi b. <strong>1998</strong> &mdash; the expansion is accelerating. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>2015</strong> &mdash; LIGO hears two black holes merge. <strong>2019</strong> &mdash; M87* is photographed. <strong>2022</strong> &mdash; so is Sgr A*, and JWST opens its eye. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

