<!-- ============================== GLOSSARY ================================ -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-the-words">The Words</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Light year</dt><dd>The distance light travels in a year: 9.46 trillion km. A distance, never a time.</dd>
      <dt>Parsec</dt><dd>3.26 light years &mdash; the distance at which one astronomical unit subtends one arcsecond. The professional's unit, because it falls straight out of a parallax measurement.</dd>
      <dt>Magnitude</dt><dd>Brightness on a backwards logarithmic scale inherited from Hipparchus: smaller is brighter, five magnitudes is a factor of a hundred. <em>Apparent</em> is how bright it looks; <em>absolute</em> is how bright it would look at 10 parsecs.</dd>
      <dt>Metallicity</dt><dd>To an astronomer, every element heavier than helium is a metal. A star's metallicity tells you which generation it belongs to.</dd>
      <dt>Schwarzschild radius</dt><dd>The horizon radius of a non-rotating black hole: about 3 km per solar mass. The Earth's would be 9 mm.</dd>
      <dt>Redshift (z)</dt><dd>The stretching of light by the expansion of space. z&nbsp;=&nbsp;1 means the universe has doubled in size since that light set out.</dd>
      <dt>Standard candle</dt><dd>An object whose true brightness is known, so its distance follows from how faint it looks. Cepheids and type Ia supernovae.</dd>
      <dt>Accretion disc</dt><dd>Matter spiralling in, shedding angular momentum, and heating by friction until it shines &mdash; the most efficient energy source known, up to 40% of rest mass against hydrogen fusion's 0.7%.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

